 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Since Pieter Mulier left Alaïa for Versace, the team he left behind has been designing the collection. The Maison asked the audience to the Fondation Cartier for a semi-informal viewing of their first collection without a named pilot at the helm. We stood to watch.</p><p>Perhaps it’s a wait-and-see policy on the management’s side: rather than immediately searching for another known star to take over, maybe this season is an internal test, a try-out to see what the reaction to the team’s work may be in stores, and maybe from the press.</p><p>Existing Alaïa customers may register no change. The collection respectfully continued with the familiar house staples: the bodysuit, the leather tailoring, the fitted, flippy-skirted mini dresses and glamorous coats. The designers made a feature of the diagonally-angled tiered, pleated skirt: it appeared in leather versions, and later refined in a fluid micro-plissé technique on cocktail dresses whose density graduated from dark in the bodice to light as it flared to the hems.</p><p>Mulier always took care to acknowledge and praise the expertise of his behind-the-scenes team at Alaïa. He, and all of the generation of creative directors who’ve worked around the fashion industry for years, understand very well the destabilizing human impact on teams during the constant movement of chief designers from house to house that’s constantly happening today.</p><p>The press tends to expect drama and storytelling in shows and is quick to rush to judgement. In Alaïa’s case right now, so soon after Mulier’s departure, it seems fairer to wait a moment and see what happens.</p></div>",
